================
VLAN Hopping Prevention

Why we did this
================
After implementing VLAN Segmentation, administrators discovered that attackers could sometimes gain access to VLANs they were not authorized to access.
This attack is known as VLAN Hopping.

VLAN Hopping allows an attacker connected to one VLAN to send traffic into another VLAN without proper authorization.

The two most common VLAN Hopping techniques are:

1. Switch Spoofing Attack
   - Attacker pretends to be a switch.
   - DTP negotiation forms a trunk.
   - Attacker gains access to multiple VLANs.

2. Double Tagging Attack
   - Attacker sends frames with two VLAN tags.
   - First switch removes the outer tag.
   - Frame enters another VLAN unexpectedly.

To prevent these attacks, multiple Layer 2 security controls must be implemented together.

What will happen
================
1. Access ports are forced into Access Mode.
2. DTP negotiation is disabled.
3. Native VLAN is changed from VLAN 1.
4. Unused ports are disabled.
5. Trunk ports are manually configured.
6. Unauthorized devices cannot create trunk connections.
7. VLAN Hopping attack opportunities are significantly reduced.

Example

Without Protection
------------------

Attacker PC
     |
     |
Switch Port (Dynamic Auto)

Attacker Spoofs Switch
         ↓

Trunk Created

Result:
Access to Multiple VLANs


With Protection
---------------

Attacker PC
     |
     |
Access Port (Static Access)

DTP Disabled

Result:
No Trunk Formation
No VLAN Hopping

Advantages and Disadvantages of this, hence what came next
==========================================================

Advantages
----------
1. Prevents Switch Spoofing attacks.
2. Reduces Double Tagging attacks.
3. Improves VLAN isolation.
4. Strengthens trunk security.
5. Follows Cisco security best practices.
6. Easy to implement.
7. Reduces unauthorized VLAN access.

Disadvantages
-------------
1. Requires proper switch configuration.
2. Must be applied consistently across the network.
3. Does not stop attacks occurring within the same VLAN.
4. Configuration mistakes can affect connectivity.
5. Requires administrative effort in large environments.

Hence what came next
--------------------
Even after VLAN Hopping was prevented, devices inside the same VLAN could still attack each other.

For example:

Guest PC 1 and Guest PC 2 may belong to the same VLAN.

Although they are in the same VLAN, they should not be able to communicate with each other.

This led to:

Private VLAN (PVLAN)

which provides isolation between devices inside the same VLAN.

One-Shot Configuration
======================

Create Native VLAN

Switch(config)# vlan 999
Switch(config-vlan)# name NATIVE_VLAN


Configure Access Ports

Switch(config)# interface range fa0/1 - 24
Switch(config-if-range)# switchport mode access
Switch(config-if-range)# switchport nonegotiate


Configure Trunk Ports

Switch(config)# interface g0/1
Switch(config-if)# switchport mode trunk
Switch(config-if)# switchport trunk native vlan 999
Switch(config-if)# switchport nonegotiate


Disable Unused Ports

Switch(config)# interface range fa0/20 - 24
Switch(config-if-range)# switchport access vlan 999
Switch(config-if-range)# shutdown

Verification
============

show interfaces trunk

show interfaces switchport

show vlan brief

Expected Result
===============

Access Ports:
-------------
Static Access Mode

Trunk Ports:
------------
Static Trunk Mode

DTP:
----
Disabled

Native VLAN:
------------
999

Unused Ports:
-------------
